<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>WinExe</OutputType>
    <RootNamespace>Roboc.App</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyName>Roboc</AssemblyName>
    <ApplicationManifest>app.manifest</ApplicationManifest>
    <ApplicationIcon>..\..\assets\icons\Roboc.ico</ApplicationIcon>

    <!--
      Portable single-file by default, so a plain `dotnet publish -c Release`
      produces a working deliverable.

      NativeAOT would start faster (roughly 30-50 ms against the 95-123 ms
      measured for this configuration) but its platform linker requires the
      Visual Studio C++ build tools, which are not installed here. Leaving
      PublishAot=true would make the default publish command fail with
      "Platform linker not found", so the choice is made explicitly rather than
      left as a build-breaking default. Set -p:PublishAot=true on the command
      line once the toolchain is present.
    -->
    <PublishAot>false</PublishAot>

    <OptimizationPreference>Speed</OptimizationPreference>
    <DebugType>embedded</DebugType>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Portable-packaging properties apply only when a runtime identifier is given,
    i.e. during publish.

    Setting SelfContained unconditionally also changed what a plain `dotnet build`
    produced: the output moved to a RID-specific folder while the framework-
    dependent copy at the old path stayed behind. Running that stale binary
    against freshly edited source is a confusing way to lose an afternoon: the
    build reports success and the behaviour never changes.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup Condition="'$(RuntimeIdentifier)' != ''">
    <SelfContained>true</SelfContained>
    <PublishSingleFile>true</PublishSingleFile>

    <PublishTrimmed>true</PublishTrimmed>
    <TrimMode>partial</TrimMode>
    <!--
      Required to create .lnk shortcuts for the SendTo menu. Trimming disables
      built-in COM by default, and the failure surfaces as NotSupportedException
      at the call site rather than at build time.
    -->
    <BuiltInComInteropSupport>true</BuiltInComInteropSupport>
    <EnableCompressionInSingleFile>true</EnableCompressionInSingleFile>

    <!--
      Trim warnings that are accepted rather than fixed.

      28 of them appear on a clean publish, and have done since long before the
      drawing layer changed. They are listed here, one reason per code, because
      the alternative (leaving them in the build log) is how a real warning goes
      unread. Rule 8 of the developer document says the build must be silent, and
      silence has to mean something: anything suppressed here is suppressed
      deliberately, with a reason, and any NEW code still shows up.

      IL2072 / IL2075 (26, all in Cli/ExplorerSelection.cs)
        Reading the selection out of a running Explorer window. Explorer's
        Shell.Application object is reached by ProgID and its members by name,
        because no interop assembly for it survives trimming. The whole point of
        that code is to call methods that cannot be known at compile time.
        Trimming cannot verify what it cannot see, so it warns. This is not a
        latent defect; it is the design, and it is why the selection can be read
        at all when a hundred files are selected.

      IL2026 (1, ComActivator.GetClassFactoryForTypeInternal)
        Raised by the framework itself once BuiltInComInteropSupport is on,
        which it must be (see the note above about SendTo shortcuts). There is
        no call site to annotate; the warning comes from inside the runtime.

      IL2104 (1, System.Private.Windows.Core)
        An aggregate: "this assembly produced trim warnings". It is the GDI+
        package reporting on itself, not on any line of code here.

      What is deliberately NOT listed: any warning outside these three codes.
      A new one is a new fact about this program and should be read.
    -->
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);IL2026;IL2072;IL2075;IL2104</NoWarn>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Roboc.Core\Roboc.Core.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="System.Drawing.Common" Version="10.0.12" />
  </ItemGroup>



</Project>
